 "verdict": "UNDERPOWERED, NOT NULL: rho=+0.552 in the predicted direction, p=0.1072 at n=10. An effect this size would need about n=16 to reach significance, and n here is capped by how many models concentrate at all -- nine of nineteen never do. The correct statement is that this test cannot decide, NOT that there is no association. Reporting it either way would be reading a threshold rather than the data. SEPARATELY, and this part IS clean: models that never concentrate average rep_4=0.577 against 0.581 for those that do -- indistinguishable. So the attractor BINARY has no predictive value for repetition at all; whatever signal exists is in T* as a graded quantity within the concentrating group, not in whether a model concentrates.",
 "_analysis_provenance": {
  "script": "degeneration_vs_tstar.py",
  "sha256": "f7568b8f7aac2652365cc47568502fd725c117e7731a4e935a6cd18e82101669",
  "note": "sha256 of the analysis source at write time. If this does not match the file on disk, the analysis was produced by a different version of the code -- re-run it before reading the numbers (issue #38)."
 },
 "_note": "Tests whether T* -- the temperature at which the CA's settled lattice stops being dominated by one token, interpolated from the nineteen-model screen -- predicts neural text degeneration, an independently studied failure mode. T* is tighter within a family than the raw share at any fixed temperature and separates families the attractor binary lumps together, but it is measured in a two-token regime F66 showed to be an artifact, so on its own it is a property of out-of-distribution behaviour. The anchor shares no machinery with the ring CA: greedy decoding from short but REAL sentence openings, with repetition measured as the fraction of repeated 4-grams. Greedy so there is no temperature knob to tune. A null is pre-registered as closing the question.",
